---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import { FEATURED } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { linkClass, ext } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Notes", path: "/brief" },
  { name: FEATURED.title, path: FEATURED.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/new-register";
const hero = FEATURED.image;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${FEATURED.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={FEATURED.dek}
  path={FEATURED.path}
  ogImagePath={hero}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: FEATURED.title,
      description: FEATURED.dek,
      datePublished: FEATURED.date,
      dateModified: FEATURED.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${hero}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
    >
      {FEATURED.kicker}
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {FEATURED.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone-700 mb-6">{FEATURED.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={FEATURED.dateLabel} read={FEATURED.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-stone-200">
      <img
        src={withBase(hero)}
        alt={FEATURED.imageAlt}
        width="1536"
        height="1024"
        class="w-full object-cover object-top max-h-80"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-stone-500">
        Gearfire Point of Sale, on the counter.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        Gearfire launched Gearfire Point of Sale today.{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.theoutdoorwire.com/releases/2026/08/gearfire-launches-cloud-based-pos-built-for-specialty-outdoor-retailers/"
          class={linkClass}
          {...ext}
          >The Outdoor Wire has the release</a
        >. GPOS is available now, starting at $149 a month, with FastBound, a
        catalog of more than 150,000 items, and live inventory with Gearfire
        eCommerce. POS is a competitive niche. Gearfire is a major player. That
        is the occasion. It is not the story.
      </p>
      <p>They debuted it at Kinsey’s in February. The register is in market now.</p>
      <p>The story is the three numbers. A new register does not rewrite them.</p>
      <p>
        A register is for ringing what sold. It is good at that. It is supposed
        to be. A better register is still a register. It sees the gun that went
        out the door. It does not see the search that came back empty. Call that
        the miss if you want: an empty search. Empty never rings.
      </p>
      <p>That is not a knock. It is the job.</p>
      <p>
        Gearfire already drew the first cut: a background check is not a gun
        sale. Permits, used guns, transfers, denials, two guns on one check.
        Counting what rang is better than counting FBI checks. They were right.
        They built an index on it. Same stores. New guns only. This year against
        last year. They revise it when more shops finish sending. Useful. It is
        last month. It is still only what sold.
      </p>
      <p>
        RetailBI is a byproduct of retail ops. You run a counter, you get a
        sales number. You run enough counters, you get an index. That number
        cannot see a model that was not on the shelf. We do not forecast last
        month. We see what they searched, including empty. We will not park a
        made-up Betsy percentage next to the Index and pretend they are the same
        job. OEMs:{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.gunsearchengine.com/demand-intelligence"
          class={linkClass}
          {...ext}>Demand Intelligence</a
        >. How the two sit side by side lives on{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.gunsearchengine.com/betsy-vs-retailbi"
          class={linkClass}
          {...ext}>Betsy vs RetailBI</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Betsy is a byproduct of product discovery. Someone hunted a gun on a
        store site. They found it, or they did not. Both happened. Only one of
        those becomes a unit sold. Google Analytics never named the empty
        search. A session is not “they hunted a model you did not have.”
      </p>
      <p>
        The sensor sits on the site the dealer already has. Including a Gearfire
        storefront. Dealers:{" "}
        <a href="https://gunsearchagent.com" class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >GunSearchAgent</a
        >. It does not replace the register. It does not replace the Index. It
        writes down the search.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a POS recap. It is not a scorecard of who rings faster. It
        is not gossip about which stack a shop should buy. Demand is a different
        number.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
      >
        Three numbers. Stop mixing these up.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        Big news is when those three move. A product launch in a competitive
        niche is an occasion. It is not big news. A new register does not change
        checks, sell-through, or search intent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800 mb-4"
      >
        What to do next
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <a
          href="https://www.gunsearchengine.com/demand-intelligence"
          class="group rounded-2xl border border-rule bg-white p-5 shadow-sm hover:border-sky-600/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all"
          {...ext}
        >
          <p
            class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800"
          >
            If you make the guns
          </p>
          <h2
            class="mt-2 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink group-hover:text-sky-900"
          >
            OEM / DI
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">
            The Index counts new guns that sold at the same stores. It cannot
            see a search for a gun that was not on the shelf. Betsy BI sits next
            to RetailBI. It does not replace it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-medium text-sky-800">
            Demand Intelligence →
          </p>
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://gunsearchagent.com"
          class="group rounded-2xl border border-rule bg-white p-5 shadow-sm hover:border-sky-600/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all"
          {...ext}
        >
          <p
            class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800"
          >
            If you sell them
          </p>
          <h2
            class="mt-2 font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink group-hover:text-sky-900"
          >
            Dealer / GSA
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">
            A session is not an empty search. GunSearchAgent sits on your store
            site, next to Google Analytics or instead of it, and writes the
            search down. The site you already have. Whatever is ringing the
            drawer.
          </p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p
      class="mt-10 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif"
    >
      <a href="https://fflintel.com" class={linkClass}>FFL Intel</a> is the
      journal of the three numbers.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-stone-500">
      Published by Coriolis, LLC. Numbers by Betsy. Not affiliated with
      Gearfire or RetailBI.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
